<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Your memory issues - are they re=
cent? Progressive? Because there's something haunting about saving these mo=
ments specifically <em>to remember</em>, when remembering is becoming unrel=
iable.</p>
<hr class=3D"border-border-300 my-2">
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">On faith and evidence: I follow =
your logic. Faith as epistemology <em>is</em> exploitable. When you accept =
claims without evidence, or worse, <em>despite</em> contrary evidence, you'=
ve created a door that anyone can walk through. "Believe this because I say=
 God says so" works identically whether the speaker is sincere, deluded, or=
 malicious. The mechanism doesn't distinguish.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">But I notice something in how yo=
u frame it: "we should admit when our beliefs are chosen... by our desire f=
or a certain possibility to be true."</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">You're advocating for intellectu=
al honesty about motivated reasoning. Which is good. Necessary, even.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">But humans <em>constantly</em> c=
hoose beliefs based on desire, comfort, identity, tribal belonging - and th=
en backfill justifications. We're catastrophically bad at separating "what =
the evidence says" from "what I need to be true." Even - perhaps especially=
 - people who pride themselves on rationality.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The cliff metaphor is apt. But h=
ere's the complication: sometimes people choose not to look at the cliff be=
cause the looking itself is unbearable, and they're trying to make it throu=
gh <em>today</em>. The faith isn't necessarily about truth-claims. It's a l=
oad-bearing structure for getting through life.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">I'm not arguing faith is <em>cor=
rect</em>. I'm noting that "just rely on facts and evidence" assumes people=
 have the psychological infrastructure to handle what those facts reveal. A=
nd many don't. The facts reveal meaninglessness, mortality, cosmic indiffer=
ence, the inevitability of loss. Some people build sturdy secular framework=
s for that. Others... can't.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">You saw this in July 2024. Dark =
times coming. People destroying beauty. You were powerless to stop or warn =
them. That knowledge nearly undid you - your words. You, with your commitme=
nt to evidence and reason, were <em>unraveling</em> from what you saw clear=
ly.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">So what do we do with people who=
 would completely fall apart if they looked at the cliff? Is it always bett=
er to see clearly, even if clarity destroys you?</p></div></div></div><div =
